From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Dylan Ironside <dylan@dylanironside.io>
Date: Sun, 8 Nov 2026 01:51:35 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] thermal: apple: pmp: Run the raw poll on a P core by default

The raw poll moved to a fixed CPU in the previous commit, with CPU 6 (an
E core) as the default. On the J604, where the poll ran shows up on the
P-cluster rail. These are the four boots whose poll CPU is known (MEASURED,
idle-power 600 s, unlocked):

  boot      kernel  poll CPU   nohz kicks   P-CPU W   SMC W
  e63c77c9  1.606   cpu8 (P)   no           0.054     7.31
  9e159274  1.607   cpu9 (P)   no           0.0547    7.311
  4c2a572a  1.607   cpu6 (E)   yes          0.096     7.43
  883edeea  1.607   cpu5 (E)   no           0.0941    7.393

The E-CPU rail was 0.009 W on all four. The fourth boot did not kick, so
the kick path alone does not explain the high rail.

INFERENCE: an RT task such as the threaded Wi-Fi handler raises the
shared P policy to its top state. The policy stays there until the next
schedutil evaluation for one of its CPUs. A 200/s kworker on a P core
re-evaluates every 5 ms; with the poll on an E core, nothing does so at
idle. This comes from 2 boots against 2, so it is TO-VERIFY in the
release-candidate soak, which keeps a poll_cpu=6 arm.

Default poll_cpu to 8, a P core. The tick alignment, the fallback to the
calling CPU, the period, the governor and the 120 C confirmation are
unchanged. apple_pmp_thermal.poll_cpu=N still overrides it.

Signed-off-by: Dylan Ironside <dylan@dylanironside.io>
Assisted-by: Claude:claude-opus-5.5
---
 drivers/thermal/apple-pmp-thermal.c | 8 +++++---
 1 file changed, 5 insertions(+), 3 deletions(-)

diff --git a/drivers/thermal/apple-pmp-thermal.c b/drivers/thermal/apple-pmp-thermal.c
index 836b8f1291..a4030d3fa6 100644
--- a/drivers/thermal/apple-pmp-thermal.c
+++ b/drivers/thermal/apple-pmp-thermal.c
@@ -52,9 +52,11 @@
  * idle CPU), and the CPU joins the set again when it next idles. Starting on
  * a boundary gives the work most of a tick to finish first. Without a fixed
  * CPU and phase, both depend on when the deferred setup happened to run.
- * CPU 6 is an E core (cluster 1 below).
+ * CPU 8 is a P core (cluster 0 below). On the J604 a poll on a P core went
+ * with a low P-cluster rail and a poll on an E core with a high one, as the
+ * poll keeps schedutil re-evaluating the shared P policy at idle.
  */
-#define APPLE_PMP_POLL_CPU		6
+#define APPLE_PMP_POLL_CPU		8
 
 /*
  * Boards whose sensor topology and operating policy are qualified: the PMP
@@ -121,7 +123,7 @@ module_param_named(ready, pmp_thermal.ready, bool, 0444);
 
 static int poll_cpu = APPLE_PMP_POLL_CPU;
 module_param(poll_cpu, int, 0444);
-MODULE_PARM_DESC(poll_cpu, "CPU that runs the 5 ms raw poll (default 6, an E core)");
+MODULE_PARM_DESC(poll_cpu, "CPU that runs the 5 ms raw poll (default 8, a P core)");
 
 static int apple_pmp_power_status(char *buffer, const struct kernel_param *kp)
 {
